# Virtual Pat Brief — Quiet-by-Design at MWAA

**Author lens:** engineer-tinkerer. First question is never "what should we buy?" — it is "what does the airport already own that nobody is using this way?" I am writing to make sure the Strategist always has the version of the argument that costs 2% as much and ships 20x sooner.

**Scope of this brief:** the asset inventory, five ordered solutions built out of things MWAA already owns, precedents that already run something like each solution, three 90-day pilots that would generate real evidence before the next Concourse C/D NTP, and the honest kill list.

---

## 1. Asset Inventory — What MWAA Already Owns That Bears On This

- **The MWAA Design Manual (2020, seven volumes) plus Specifications, plus the MASTERSPEC editing infrastructure the Office of Engineering already runs.** MWAA describes the Manual as a mandatory guide with the force of law on airport property, updated annually. That is the delivery vehicle. Standards do not need to become policy resolutions or Board-adopted "programs"; they need to become an amended Design Manual volume and three or four new specification sections. This asset is worth more than any technology in the room, and it is fully paid for.
- **FIDS at every gate, on every concourse, and at baggage claim.** US DOT accessibility rules already require that audio pages be available as visual pages. That means every airport dollar spent on FIDS already carries latent capacity to be the primary passenger information channel. What is missing is a policy that treats FIDS as primary and overhead PA as exception, not the other way around.
- **The two dominant airline apps at MWAA's two airports.** United (IAD anchor) and American (DCA anchor) already push gate changes and boarding-status updates directly to passengers' phones. That is a redundant, personalized, multilingual information channel MWAA neither owns nor pays for. What is missing is a tenant standard that makes it the primary passenger-notification channel and demotes the overhead PA.
- **Airline gate-podium PAs.** Airlines already have local speakers. The problem is not a lack of local audio; it is that the centralized overhead PA repeats what the podium just said, at a different level, on a different delay, over a different acoustic path — the classic double-broadcast that destroys speech transmission at the very moment the passenger needs to hear it.
- **The Concourse E opening (fall 2026) and every Concourse C/D and DCA T1 replacement package still upstream of NTP.** These are the commissioning windows in which a quiet-by-design standard is nearly free to instrument. After NTP they are change orders.
- **The 2025 Airline Use & Lease Agreement (15-year term).** Not a blank slate — a defined tenant-standards attachment mechanism. Standards can be amended into that framework without reopening the base agreement.
- **The MWAA Operations Center, MWAA Police dispatch, and the existing NFPA 72-compliant fire/mass-notification stack.** These are the "safety-critical" channel. They have to stay loud, redundant, and code-compliant. Every other audio pathway can be redesigned around them, not merged with them.

The important observation: the assets exist. The gap is a governance decision to point them at "quiet by design" as a defined performance requirement instead of as a marketing sensibility.

