## 2. Five Solutions, Ordered Most-to-Least Achievable

### Solution 1 — Amend the Design Manual before the next NTP; do not build a "program"

**Problem attacked:** every standard MWAA does not codify before Concourse C/D notice-to-proceed will be argued as a change order. Standards adopted late cost 10-40x standards adopted early.

**Mechanism:** open the 2020 Design Manual's technology and architectural volumes, add a "Quiet-by-Design" section and four to six new specification sections (acoustic ceilings, room criteria, PA zoning, STI commissioning, tenant audio limits, quiet-space design). Adopt the ACRP Report 175 methodology as the reference basis — it is the domestic authoritative document and MWAA does not have to invent the science.

**Recombination:** the Manual apparatus, the MASTERSPEC editing workflow, and the annual update cycle. All three already exist.

**Cost:** low six figures for a consultant editor plus MWAA staff time. Nothing procured. No capex.

**Path:** technical amendment, Chief Engineer signature, annual issuance. No Board resolution required if framed as a design standard, not a policy.

**Resistance:** consulting engineers who quote from experience and who resist prescriptive commissioning language because it exposes their work to STI measurement. Handle by writing the STI thresholds at STI ≥ 0.50 for passenger-processing areas and STI ≥ 0.45 for concourse general boarding — numbers already sitting in ACRP 175 and NFPA 72 Ch. 24, so the argument is not with MWAA.

**Failure mode:** the amendment gets written, sits in Volume 5, and nobody enforces it at the 50% design review because the plan-review checklist was not updated. Fix by amending the checklist in the same package.

### Solution 2 — Put SFO's tenant sound clause verbatim into the next concession lease template

**Problem attacked:** roughly 30-40% of ambient sound in a modern terminal comes from tenants — televisions, background music, sample-play kiosks, coffee grinders, radio-station kiosks. No acoustic ceiling and no PA zoning fixes this. Only the lease does.

**Mechanism:** SFO has published concession sound-footprint language. Copy it into the next MWAA concession lease template and into the tenant Design Criteria. Cap tenant audio at a metered LEQ at the lease-line, not at the source. Require muted televisions in gate-adjacent airline clubs.

**Recombination:** existing lease templates + existing tenant design-criteria manuals + existing plan-review process at fit-out.

**Cost:** legal review, roughly $30-75K. Nothing procured.

**Path:** next standard-lease revision cycle. Grandfather in-place tenants; enforce at first renewal or first major fit-out. Real coverage in five to seven years.

**Resistance:** concessions and airline-club operators. Argument is empirical — SFO's 40% reduction in on-site noise came in significant part from this clause, not from the PA reprogramming.

**Failure mode:** exceptions get granted casually. Fix by requiring the Real Estate Committee to see every exception in aggregate, quarterly.

### Solution 3 — Make the airline app the primary boarding channel; demote overhead PA to exception

**Problem attacked:** the PA fires because the gate agent has no faster way to reach the passengers. This is untrue since roughly 2019. United and American both push boarding-status notifications automatically to app users; the average boarding-pass-holder at a MWAA gate is carrying the device that would receive them.

**Mechanism:** rewrite the MWAA Airline Operating Standards to require, at every gate, (a) a real-time app boarding push, (b) a full FIDS-text overlay ("BOARDING GROUP 3 — GATE C21"), and (c) directional-speaker or podium PA audio, in that order. The centralized overhead PA is reserved for irregular operations, safety, and passenger-name paging by airport staff, not routine boarding calls. Gate agents keep local audio at the podium; that is not what we are turning off.

**Recombination:** airline apps that already exist and cost MWAA nothing; FIDS that already exist and are already required to be capable of visual paging; podium speakers that airlines already own.

**Cost:** near zero capex. The cost is the Operating Standards amendment and the airline-relations lift.

**Path:** amend Airline Operating Standards; work with United (IAD) and American (DCA) on a joint pilot at one concourse each. Both carriers already have the underlying app functionality; the pilot is about disciplining the gate-agent workflow, not about building new software.

**Resistance:** airlines fear no-shows and misconnects during irregular operations. Legitimate concern. Answer: overhead PA remains the fallback and stays wired the same way. The pilot measures whether no-show rates change; they almost certainly do not, because the passenger who missed the boarding call almost certainly also missed the same information on FIDS, on the app, and on the podium PA. Missed boardings are usually a passenger-behavior problem, not a signal-path problem.

**Failure mode:** the wrong metric wins the argument. The metric that matters is D0 completion rate and gate-hold-back duration, not "did we page." If the airline data show equivalent performance, the case closes.

### Solution 4 — Directional gate speakers plus a hard cap on overhead PA zones

**Problem attacked:** the physics of the double-broadcast. A gate PA firing at the same instant as an overhead PA cancels the intelligibility of both. Directional loudspeakers (parametric arrays that project a narrow beam of audible sound) contain audio within the gate hold-room and stop the acoustic spill into the adjacent hold-room.

**Mechanism:** at each gate, install one Holosonics AS-168iX-class parametric speaker (list price roughly $1,300 per unit) aimed into the hold-room from the podium canopy. Then, in the PA head-end, redefine the announcement zones so that overhead PA zones stop at the airside/landside boundary and at every gate hold-room boundary. Zone count roughly doubles; per-zone content becomes much lighter.

**Recombination:** parametric-speaker technology (mature, retail-priced), existing PA head-end (already zone-capable in every modern airport DSP), gate architecture (already delineated).

**Cost:** unit hardware roughly $1,300; installed cost per gate roughly $3,500-6,000 including power, low-voltage, DSP integration, and commissioning. At IAD's roughly 100+ gates that is a $400-700K program; at DCA's roughly 50 gates roughly $200-300K. Total under $1M across both airports. That is a rounding error against the $20B+ IAD transformation, and it is a one-time capex.

**Path:** commission at Concourse E during fall 2026 opening as the first live installation. Roll to Concourse C/D as those spaces come online. Retrofit older concourses opportunistically.

**Resistance:** parametric speakers have real caveats — narrow beam pattern, sensitivity to reflective surfaces behind the listener, limited low-frequency response. These are not disqualifying; they are design inputs. Wilson Ihrig and other airport acousticians have working knowledge of the technology; the Design Manual amendment can specify where directional works and where it does not.

**Failure mode:** parametric speaker installed pointing at a glass storefront and echoes hilariously. Handle by commissioning STI measurement at each installed unit.

### Solution 5 — Retrofit sensory rooms out of underused back-of-house space; certify with KultureCity

**Problem attacked:** the accessibility case for "quiet by design" cannot rest on absent announcements. It has to include a place a passenger with sensory-processing needs, a nonverbal child, or a person having a panic attack can actually go. IAD and DCA both have vacant retail bays and closed carrier-service kiosks post-Sprint / post-Verizon-store that have been unrented for months. That is airside real estate MWAA already owns and is already lighting and conditioning.

**Mechanism:** convert two to four such bays per airport into 200-400 sq ft sensory rooms — acoustic isolation, dimmable lighting, no windows to the concourse, KultureCity signage, sensory bags at the info desks. KultureCity's certification model is turnkey and covers 3,000+ venues including airports (IND is a live example).

**Recombination:** existing vacant airside real estate + existing FF&E budget + KultureCity's off-the-shelf certification pathway + existing customer-service staffing.

**Cost:** roughly $50-100K per room installed, plus roughly $5K annual for KultureCity support. Four rooms across both airports is under $500K one-time and under $25K annual.

**Path:** identify vacant bays now; scope one at each airport in the FY27 minor-capital budget; certify by end of 2027.

**Resistance:** concessions team wants the vacant bay back on the RFP. Answer: the airline U&L agreement's amenities framework and MWAA's own passenger-experience KPIs create a defensible offset. If the bay's realistic re-lease value is $80-150K/yr, MWAA can carry the room instead and score against accessibility performance measures.

**Failure mode:** the room is unstaffed and becomes a place people sleep. Handle by camera monitoring from the ops center and by locating adjacent to a staffed information desk.

