## 3. Precedents — Operations Already Running Something Like Each Solution

- **SFO Quiet Airport (Solutions 1, 2, 3).** SFO reworked its PA head-end in 2019 to broadcast gate announcements only in the immediate area around the departing flight; worked with airlines to cut roughly 40% of paging; wrote concessionaire sound-footprint rules. Result reported as 90 minutes/day of announcement time removed and roughly 40% on-site noise reduction. Reporting is secondary and the reduction methodology was not primary-sourced in this brief; the reported result nonetheless represents the closest US large-hub precedent. [Source: https://viewfromthewing.com/san-francisco-airport-removed-90-minutes-of-daily-noise-travelers-say-it-changed-everything/]

- **London City Airport silent-airport policy (Solutions 3, 4).** Announcements only at gates except in true emergencies; flight communications relayed via FIDS, silent kiosks, text, and mobile apps. [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/at-the-airport/need-to-know/airport-policies/silent-policy]

- **Helsinki-Vantaa quiet airport since 2015 (Solutions 3, 4).** No flight or passenger announcements in the terminal outside gate areas and emergencies; FIDS-primary information architecture. [Source: https://morepremium.com/helsinki-vantaa-to-be-made-a-quiet-airport/]

- **Indianapolis International Airport KultureCity certification (Solution 5).** First Indiana airport to receive sensory certification; two post-security sensory rooms; sensory bags at information desks; staff training. [Source: https://www.ind.com/about/media/media-releases/indy-airport-is-now-a-certified-sensory-inclusive-venue]

- **Minneapolis-Saint Paul visual paging (Solution 3 + accessibility floor).** All audio pages made from MSP's paging office are simultaneously available as text on FIDS-family screens across both terminals. [Source: https://www.mspairport.com/airport/deaf-and-hard-hearing]

- **Non-aviation analog: NFPA 72 Chapter 24 for mass notification (foundational for all solutions).** Sets a minimum intelligibility threshold of STI 0.45 for emergency communications, distinguishes audibility from intelligibility, and encourages multilingual messages. This is a domestic code baseline that a quiet-by-design standard has to sit on top of, not underneath. [Source: https://www.alertus.com/blog/nfpa-72-mass-notification-requirements-guide]

