## 4. The "Do It By Thursday" Tier — Three Pilots for Under $100K, Ninety Days

**Pilot A — Baseline commissioning at Concourse E opening.** Take Concourse E acceptance testing already scheduled for fall 2026 and add a Class 1 sound level meter with an STI module (roughly $15-20K, or borrowed from the acoustical engineer of record for zero). Run STI, ambient LEQ, and reverberation-time surveys at 20 measurement points on opening week. Cost: staff time plus meter rental. Output: the first defensible baseline data set MWAA has ever owned. Every future project has something to be measured against.

**Pilot B — "Gate paging holiday" at two DCA gates for a 30-day window.** Turn off the overhead PA (not the podium PA, not the emergency channel, not the FIDS overlay, not the airline apps) at two adjacent gates for one full airline operating day per week. Instrument via the airline's D0 completion rate, gate-agent count of "help I missed my flight" incidents, and passenger-experience survey delta. Cost: coordination time with American and MWAA Ops; no procurement. Output: airline-owned empirical answer to "will people miss flights."

**Pilot C — Two parametric speakers at Concourse E gates.** Install one Holosonics AS-168iX at each of two Concourse E gates on opening. Instrument with an STI reading inside the hold-room and 15 feet into the adjacent hold-room. Cost: under $10K in hardware plus installation labor. Output: a photograph-ready before/after that Council members and Board members can hear during a site visit. Nothing sells a design standard like standing in a hold-room and having someone talk quietly to you while the next gate is invisible.

All three pilots share one property: they generate evidence that either kills or accelerates the full-scale case. Neither of them requires an RFP, a Board vote, or a program manager. Together they cost less than the graphics budget for a single Board presentation.

