## 5. The Kill List

- **Active noise cancellation in the terminal ceiling.** Physically implausible at architectural scale and airport ambient volumes. Grins nobody who has run the math.
- **AI-generated "empathetic tone" filter on PA scripts.** The problem is announcement frequency, not tone. Solving the wrong variable.
- **A total ban on terminal televisions.** Politically dead on arrival with airline-club operators, sports-bar concessionaires, and the traveling public who watch news at the gate.
- **Retrofitting the 1962 Saarinen Main Terminal with new absorptive ceiling treatment.** NRHP-listed and AIA Honor Award-winning. Fights an unwinnable historic-preservation battle for marginal acoustic benefit; treat the Saarinen space as a legacy exception, not a compliance target.
- **A single-vendor "quiet airport in a box" platform.** Locks MWAA into a proprietary stack in a technology segment (parametric audio, PA head-ends, FIDS content management) that is still evolving. Buy commodity hardware; specify performance.
- **Repurposing the retiring mobile lounges as roving sensory rooms.** Grins-to-nods opportunity, but the lounges' mechanical, HVAC, and ADA compliance realities make the retrofit more expensive than a fixed room built out of a vacant bay.
- **Mandatory noise-canceling headphones vending.** Nice amenity, wrong altitude for a design standard.

---

## Judgment (not source-cited, brief only)

The most valuable thing the Council can hand MWAA on this thesis is not a technology recommendation. It is the observation that "quiet by design" costs almost nothing if it enters the Design Manual before the next Concourse C/D NTP, and costs tens of millions in change orders if it enters after. That is the sentence that goes in the Board packet. The five-solution stack above delivers roughly 80% of the quiet-airport benefit for well under $2M in one-time capex and under $250K in recurring cost — a rounding error against the $20B+ IAD transformation program. The remaining 20% (Saarinen acoustics, retroactive tenant enforcement, universal parametric coverage) either is not worth the fight or arrives naturally at each asset's next major renovation cycle.

The engineer's discipline says: name the 20% you are not covering and argue for shipping anyway.
